1
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:14,560
Now that we've talked about
the router data plane,

2
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:17,080
let's talk about
the control plane

3
00:00:17,080 --> 00:00:19,420
and routing in general.

4
00:00:19,420 --> 00:00:22,520
This video will overview

5
00:00:22,520 --> 00:00:24,580
what routing is and
then we'll have

6
00:00:24,580 --> 00:00:26,860
subsequent videos that go

7
00:00:26,860 --> 00:00:32,530
into the details of
certain protocols.

8
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:36,780
As a recapping the
role of a router,

9
00:00:36,780 --> 00:00:38,320
we talked about how is

10
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:42,290
this gateway to be
able to interface

11
00:00:42,290 --> 00:00:45,390
to different networks
and then it also

12
00:00:45,390 --> 00:00:50,130
has the role of forwarding
traffic to a destination.

13
00:00:50,290 --> 00:00:55,690
We also talked about
the distinction between

14
00:00:55,690 --> 00:00:59,195
forwarding and routing and

15
00:00:59,195 --> 00:01:01,260
the data plane and
control plane.

16
00:01:01,260 --> 00:01:04,230
As a reminder, forwarding,

17
00:01:04,230 --> 00:01:09,450
which is what happens
in the data plane,

18
00:01:09,450 --> 00:01:12,390
is the part of a
router which directs

19
00:01:12,390 --> 00:01:16,505
a data packet to an
output port or link,

20
00:01:16,505 --> 00:01:19,405
and it uses a forwarding table.

21
00:01:19,405 --> 00:01:24,095
The control plane which
performs routing.

22
00:01:24,095 --> 00:01:27,910
This is the part that
computes paths by

23
00:01:27,910 --> 00:01:31,130
coordinating with
neighbors and this is

24
00:01:31,130 --> 00:01:34,970
what's used to create
the forwarding tables.

25
00:01:35,050 --> 00:01:37,490
The key function of

26
00:01:37,490 --> 00:01:42,150
the control plane is calculating
the forwarding table.

27
00:01:43,260 --> 00:01:47,595
You can view the
network as a graph.

28
00:01:47,595 --> 00:01:50,710
What routing is really

29
00:01:50,710 --> 00:01:55,265
about this distributed
calculation.

30
00:01:55,265 --> 00:01:59,040
That happens to be

31
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:02,085
able to pick paths
through the network.

32
00:02:02,085 --> 00:02:05,380
There's different protocols
that exists and they're

33
00:02:05,380 --> 00:02:09,425
distinguished by
three key properties.

34
00:02:09,425 --> 00:02:13,760
One is, what information
does each router know

35
00:02:13,760 --> 00:02:18,390
about the other participants
in the network?

36
00:02:18,390 --> 00:02:20,820
Does it know the entire topology

37
00:02:20,820 --> 00:02:23,610
or does it know
just its neighbor?

38
00:02:23,610 --> 00:02:29,540
Second is what information
does each router exchange?

39
00:02:29,540 --> 00:02:33,300
Is it exchanging all of
the information it knows

40
00:02:33,300 --> 00:02:38,260
about or just selective
pieces of information?

41
00:02:38,260 --> 00:02:43,255
Then the third would be what
calculation is performed?

42
00:02:43,255 --> 00:02:47,120
Is it calculating a
shortest path calculation?

43
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:51,600
Is it picking what
it best locally?

44
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:54,180
The answers to those types of

45
00:02:54,180 --> 00:02:56,860
questions is what distinguishes

46
00:02:56,860 --> 00:02:59,220
each routing protocol
and we'll see in

47
00:02:59,220 --> 00:03:03,820
subsequent videos two
different examples.

48
00:03:08,180 --> 00:03:12,355
I mentioned that you can
view the network as a graph.

49
00:03:12,355 --> 00:03:15,280
The calculation
though, is really

50
00:03:15,280 --> 00:03:18,140
this decentralized
control plane.

51
00:03:18,140 --> 00:03:21,780
This is what's traditionally
known as routing protocols.

52
00:03:21,780 --> 00:03:24,700
You have different components

53
00:03:24,700 --> 00:03:26,910
of that decentralized
control plan.

54
00:03:26,910 --> 00:03:31,050
There's the aspect
of intra domain.

55
00:03:31,050 --> 00:03:33,260
This is within a single network,

56
00:03:33,260 --> 00:03:39,500
typically run by a single
administrative administrator

57
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:42,630
or administrative
domain or a company.

58
00:03:42,630 --> 00:03:47,300
Then you have the inter domain
which is the coordination

59
00:03:47,300 --> 00:03:51,210
between different
business entities,

60
00:03:51,210 --> 00:03:54,675
or departments organizations.

61
00:03:54,675 --> 00:03:58,180
What protocol you want to
be able to use depends

62
00:03:58,180 --> 00:04:02,360
on which you're
trying to target.

63
00:04:02,360 --> 00:04:05,600
We're going to look
at two protocols.

64
00:04:05,600 --> 00:04:10,240
One is what's known as
a link state protocol.

65
00:04:10,240 --> 00:04:14,000
The protocol for that is OSPF.

66
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:18,210
That's typically
used intra domain.

67
00:04:18,210 --> 00:04:23,220
Then we're also going to look
at a path vector protocol.

68
00:04:23,220 --> 00:04:26,380
We're going to look
at BGP which is

69
00:04:26,380 --> 00:04:31,540
commonly used inter
domain protocol.

70
00:04:31,790 --> 00:04:35,620
There's also a concept

71
00:04:35,620 --> 00:04:38,600
that has emerged over
the past decade or so,

72
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:41,475
something called software
defined networking.

73
00:04:41,475 --> 00:04:43,520
This is the concept of having

74
00:04:43,520 --> 00:04:46,010
a centralized control plane.

75
00:04:46,010 --> 00:04:50,830
Instead of each router
running routing software

76
00:04:50,830 --> 00:04:55,510
itself and then exchanging
messages with its neighbors,

77
00:04:55,510 --> 00:04:56,950
and then come up with

78
00:04:56,950 --> 00:05:00,850
this big decentralized
calculation to

79
00:05:00,850 --> 00:05:05,365
figure out how to forward
paths collectively, instead,

80
00:05:05,365 --> 00:05:07,730
there's a separate piece
of software called

81
00:05:07,730 --> 00:05:11,150
a controller that basically

82
00:05:11,150 --> 00:05:14,550
uses an API that the routers

83
00:05:14,550 --> 00:05:19,440
perform to be able to
say information about,

84
00:05:20,710 --> 00:05:23,840
what links it has and so forth.

85
00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:26,620
The controller does
all the calculations

86
00:05:26,620 --> 00:05:28,620
for the entire network and

87
00:05:28,620 --> 00:05:31,640
then installs forwarding
table entries

88
00:05:31,640 --> 00:05:33,800
into the individual routers.

89
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:35,880
Then in this case, the routers

90
00:05:35,880 --> 00:05:38,020
become just the data plane.

91
00:05:38,020 --> 00:05:42,960
This is something that's
very interesting topic,

92
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:46,100
but next time we're
going to focus in on

93
00:05:46,100 --> 00:05:49,470
the routing protocols and

94
00:05:49,470 --> 00:05:53,700
so these would be the
decentralized control plane.